cat > /home/sfmqrb/git/rishe/data/verification/page-40.json <<'JSONEOF'
{
  "pdf_page": 40,
  "book_page": 10,
  "verified_by": "claude-fable-5-1",
  "verified_on": "2026-09-04",
  "entries": [
    {
      "entry": 0,
      "root": "Aik, Ěik",
      "verdict": "confirmed",
      "modern_form": "PIE *h₂eyḱ- 'to appropriate, possess, be master of' (Pokorny ēik- 298; Watkins/AHD ēik-)",
      "note": "Root, language label and gloss are all right. Pokorny IEW 298-299 heads the entry 'eik- ‚zu eigen haben, vermögen‘'; Walde-Pokorny I 105 adds the important observation that it is 'eine ausschließlich arisch-germ. Wurzel' — i.e. exactly the two branches (Indo-Iranian and Germanic) that Nourai draws, and no others. Watkins (AHD 1985) writes it 'ēik-. To be master of, possess. [Pok. ēik- 289]' (the '289' is a misprint or a scan misreading for 298). Modern Wiktionary reconstructs *h₂eyḱ- 'to appropriate, possess', with the a-vocalism of Germanic and Tocharian explained as a pervasive o-grade; Nourai's double citation form 'Aik, Ěik' reflects exactly this old a-/ē- ablaut notation. One caveat carried into node #1: Nourai's gloss 'wealthy' for Av. isvan- comes from Klein (p.1106 'isvan-, ‘well off, rich’'); Bartholomae AiW glosses isvan- 'verfügend, Herr über-, der (es) vermag' — 'able, having power over', not 'rich'.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Reconstruction:Proto-Indo-European/h%E2%82%82ey%E1%B8%B1-",
        "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Reconstruction:Proto-Germanic/aigan%C4%85"
      ],
      "ref_check": [
        {"ref": "POK:298", "status": "supports", "note": "POK_01.txt ƒƒ-leaf 311 (printed pp. 298-300) carries the entry 'eik- ,zu eigen haben, vermögen‘. Ai. ī́śe, ī́ṣṭe ... ,hat zu eigen, besitzt, beherrscht‘ ... av. iše ,ist Herr über‘, isvan- ,vermögend‘, išti- ,Gut, Reichtum‘ (germ. aihti-), aēšā- ,Vermögen; Habe‘. Hierzu got. *aigan (aih, aigum...) ,haben, besitzen‘'. The page number 298 is right (the entry begins on p.298; the running number 299 falls inside it)."},
        {"ref": "KLN:1106", "status": "supports", "note": "KLN_1966.txt ƒƒ-leaf 591, which carries the printed page numbers 1106/1107, has s.v. 'owe': 'fr. I.-E. base *aik-, ‘to own’, whence also OI. ī́śe, ‘he owns’, ... Avestic is-, ‘riches, fortune’, isvan-, ‘well off, rich’. Cp. ought, aux. v., own, adj. and v.' — Klein's base *aik- is literally Nourai's root name and gloss."}
      ],
      "consulted": [
        {"src": "POK", "where": "Bd. I p. 298-299 (POK_01.txt leaf 311)", "stance": "supports", "note": "'eik- ,zu eigen haben, vermögen‘' with the Avestan and Germanic material Nourai charts."},
        {"src": "WLD", "where": "Bd. I p. 105 (WLD_Bd1_1930_dli_70244_djvu.txt line 6850)", "stance": "supports", "note": "'eik- „zu eigen haben, vermögen“. Eine ausschließlich arisch-germ. Wurzel.' — confirms the root and, incidentally, that no Latin/Greek branch belongs here."},
        {"src": "AHD", "where": "Watkins 1985, s.v. ēik-", "stance": "supports", "note": "'ēik-. To be master of, possess. 1. Germanic *aigan, to possess, in Old English āgan, to possess: OUGHT¹, OWE. 2. Germanic participial form *aiganaz ... OWN.'"},
        {"src": "KLN", "where": "vol. II p. 1106 s.v. owe", "stance": "supports", "note": "I.-E. base *aik- 'to own', with OI. ī́śe and Avestic is-, isvan-."}
      ],
      "nodes": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "lang": "Avestan",
          "words": "îs, îše, îšti, âěšâ 1; îsvan, išvan",
          "verdict": "confirmed",
          "derivation": "All four forms are genuine Avestan continuations of PIE *h₂eyḱ-. The verb is Bartholomae's aēš- 'Herr sein über—' (AiW s.v.), a stative middle whose 3sg. is aēšē/išē 'he is master of, disposes of' — formally the Indo-Iranian perfect-stative matching Skt. ī́śe (< reduplicated *h₂e-h₂óyḱ-e), the same formation that Germanic continues in the preterite-present *aih 'I possess'. Nourai's 'îs, îše' are the zero-grade stem īš- and that 3sg. īšē; 'îšti' is the ti-abstract *Híḱ-ti- > Av. išti- f., which Bartholomae glosses '‘Vermögen, Können, Kraft’; ‘Habe, Besitz, Gut, Reichtum’' — the exact equivalent of Gothic aihts and Old English ǣht; 'âěšâ' is the full-grade *h₂oyḱ-eh₂- > Av. aēšā- 'Vermögen, Habe'; 'isvan-' is the van-adjective *Híḱ-uen- 'having power over'. The trailing '1' after 'âěšâ' is not part of the word — it is a stray superscript reference numeral picked up in the transcription. One gloss is loose: Nourai's 'wealthy' for isvan- is Klein's rendering, whereas Bartholomae gives 'verfügend, Herr über-' / 'der (es) vermögt', i.e. 'able, in command of'.",
          "sources": [
            "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Reconstruction:Proto-Indo-European/h%E2%82%82ey%E1%B8%B1-"
          ],
          "ref_check": [
